# Parallel work boundaries

Per `docs/requirements/project-requirements.md` Section 8 [NORMATIVE]: parallel work over
features is a strict rule for this project. "A situation where one person waits for another is
unacceptable and will be treated as a process defect regardless of the output."

Ported from `.claude/rules/parallel-work-boundaries.md` — keep both in sync by hand if either
changes.

## Parallelism comes from branches/worktrees, not repo boundaries

This is a true monorepo (`services/*`, `libs/*`), not git submodules. Parallel work happens via
feature branches or worktrees per contributor, not via repository boundaries.

## You are editing a shared seam right now

If you're touching a file under `libs/contracts`, `libs/config`, `docs/access-control/`, or
`docs/decisions/`, you're in the one place a slow or careless change blocks every other
contributor depending on it:

- Land shared-seam changes in small, fast-reviewed commits — don't let a contract change sit
  waiting on unrelated review cycles while other contributors are blocked on it.
- Prefer additive changes (new optional field, new DTO) over breaking ones. A breaking change to a
  shared contract is the one case where synchronous coordination (a call, not just an async PR) is
  legitimate — record the resulting decision as an ADR in `docs/decisions/` rather than letting it
  live only in chat.
- If your own service's implementation depends on a contract or API shape that doesn't exist yet,
  stub it (mock server, fixture, interface-first contract) rather than waiting for the upstream
  service to be implemented first.

## Foundation phase

Foundation-phase findings (prototyping/design approach, tech choices, testing architecture)
belong in `docs/decisions/` or `docs/integrations/` as they land, not held until a single
end-of-phase document.
